# .gitattributes — Queering Earth
#
# Two rules, both of which exist because this repository gained a second contributor on
# 2026-09-13. Before that there was one checkout, so there were no merges, no second
# machine to disagree with the first, and neither rule could ever have fired.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. NEVER NORMALISE LINE ENDINGS. This is not a style preference.
#
# The gates here verify derived files by REGENERATING THEM INTO MEMORY AND COMPARING:
# `check-metadata.mjs` reports a file that "is not what the generator writes today", and
# `make-csp.mjs --check` compares a hash of the inline snippet in every page's head.
# Git's `text=auto` rewrites line endings on checkout, per platform — so a file written
# on one machine and checked out on another could differ by exactly the bytes those gates
# compare, and every one of them would report stale on a tree nobody had touched.
#
# A stale CSP hash does not warn. The browser simply refuses the inline snippet, and every
# reader who asked for the cabinet or for plain view is shown the other thing before the
# stylesheet catches up. Store and check out as-is.
* -text

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. `search-index.json` IS 1.5 MB ON ONE LINE, and git must not try to merge it.
#
# `tools/make-search-index.mjs` writes it from the `main` landmark of all 27 pages, as one
# line.
#
# WHAT THIS RULE ACTUALLY DOES WAS MEASURED, IN A THROWAWAY CLONE, ON THE FIRST REAL
# TWO-WAY COLLISION — because the sentence inherited from Star Stuff's copy of this file
# said git might "splice two JSON documents into one that parses and is wrong", and that
# cannot happen to a file with no interior line boundaries. Merging a diverged
# `draft/*` branch with `main`, both sides having regenerated:
#
#   without this rule   git says "Auto-merging", writes CONFLICT MARKERS INTO THE FILE,
#                       and leaves 5 lines of invalid JSON
#   with this rule      git says "Cannot merge binary files", leaves the file at one
#                       line of VALID JSON (ours), and still flags the conflict
#
# So the rule does not prevent the conflict — it keeps the working file a usable index
# instead of a broken one, and `-diff` stops git rendering a 1.5 MB line as a diff.
# Neither shape is resolvable by reading, and the resolution is never by hand:
#
#     git checkout --ours search-index.json   # either side; both are about to be replaced
#     node tools/check.mjs                    # regenerates all five, in dependency order
#     git add search-index.json
#
# THE SAME REASONING COVERS EVERY OTHER DERIVED FILE — llms.txt, llms-full.txt, feed.xml,
# register.xml, the generated blocks of whats-new.html, ledger.html and what-is-settled.html,
# the CSP block in _headers, and the .md sibling of every page. They merge legibly, so they
# are left alone here, but a derived file is a COPY: two copies disagreeing is settled by
# asking the thing they are copies of, never by reading the diff.
#
# THE TRAP IS THAT A .md IS DERIVED ONLY IF A PAGE OF THAT NAME EXISTS. `ATTRIBUTIONS.md`,
# `DECISIONS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `README.md` and `ONBOARDING.md` are SOURCES, and
# `ATTRIBUTIONS.md` is the one that generates `/ledger`. Resolving it as derived drops a
# draft's credit rows while every page still reads perfectly, which is this house's
# characteristic failure arriving through a merge nobody looked at.
search-index.json -merge -diff linguist-generated=true
